  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure will continue to keep conditions pleasant\ntoday with temperatures in the upper 70s to mid 80s and low\nhumidity. Dry conditions are expected through the evening hours. \n\nA weak shortwave will move into the area tonight as the ridge\ncontinues to move to the ENE. Model soundings show pretty dry \nair across much of the area, so even though CAMs are showing \nscattered showers developing, there may not be enough moisture \navailable to actually get showers going or if they do, have it \nreach the ground. The Finger Lakes may provide enough low level \nmoisture to generate a few spotty showers, but most of the area \nshould stay dry. Lows tonight will be comfortable, falling into \nthe mid to upper 50s with some low 60s possible in the northern \nFinger Lakes and western Mohawk Valley.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe surface high pressure is now progged to be a little farther\nto the ENE, with less influence over the CWA. Dewpoints are\nexpected to climb into the low 60s by late Sunday morning as\nwinds become southerly and push some more humid air into the\nregion. This higher moisture combined with a weak trough moving\nthrough the area will kick off some scattered rain showers\nacross portions of CNY during the afternoon and early evening\nhours. A few of these showers may produce some rumbles of\nthunder, with the best chances west of I-81 where we could see\nbetter instability. Model soundings are showing very limited\ninstability east of I-81, which should hinder thunder. Severe\nweather is not expected.\n\nMuch more widespread precipitation is expected to start Monday\nafternoon. By this timeframe, we are forecast to be on the\neastern edge of the big ridge dominating the weather pattern for\nmuch of the central and western US and the western edge of a\ntrough axis centered over the Mid-Atlantic. This setup leaves \nus mostly free of the high pressure suppression and allows for \nweak shortwaves to move through the area. One of these \nshortwaves is expected to move in Monday afternoon, which \ncombined with weak instability and dewpoints in the low to mid \n60s, should kick off some scattered rain showers and isolated \nweak thunderstorms. \n\nWith the help of a jet streak running along the NE edge of the \nridge Monday night into Tuesday, a shortwave is expected to\nrapidly deepen by Tuesday. How far south this trough digs or if\nit becomes a cutoff low is still in question, as models have a \nwide range of solutions. Some show the low moving directly over\nour area while others have it pushing far to the south. In most\nsolutions however, we are forecast to see widespread rain \nshowers with isolated thunder starting Tuesday afternoon into at\nleast Thursday and possibly early Friday. Up to 2 inches of \nrain is possible during this timeframe, with higher amounts\npossible where convection develops. The timing and total \nrainfall amounts will greatly depend on where the surface low \ndevelops and tracks. We are currently in a marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall leading to flash flooding, but with the \nrecent dry spell and this looking more like widespread \nstratiform type rain with moderate rainfall rates, flooding \nchances look to be low. If the low passes more overhead or to \nour north, we could see greater instability and convection, \nwhich would produce greater rainfall rates that could lead to \nhigher flash flooding chances. Since we are not sure where the \nlow will track, a Marginal Risk seems prudent at this time. \n\nTemperatures this week should start off in the 80s on Monday.\nWe should see cooler temps through the rest of the week with\nclouds, rain, and northerly flow moving back in sometime mid-\nweek. Highs in the 70s are expected until the end of the week\nwhen warmer air will ride over the continued ridge over the\nwestern US will push temps back into the 80s for Fri/Sat.",
